 "drivingQuestion": "Why must one who vowed gold give a full dinar rather than a piece or perutot?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Both difficulties answered",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "menachot.107a.4",
   "daf": "107a",
   "sentenceId": 3,
   "he": "״הֲרֵי עָלַי זָהָב״ – לֹא יִפְחוֹת מִדִּינַר זָהָב. וְדִלְמָא נְסָכָא? אָמַר רַבִּי אֶלְעָזָר: דְּאָמַר ״מַטְבֵּעַ״. וְדִלְמָא פְּרִיטֵי? אָמַר רַב פָּפָּא: פְּרִיטֵי דְּדַהֲבָא לָא עָבְדִי אִינָשֵׁי.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches that one who says: «It» is incumbent «upon me» to donate «gold» to the Temple treasury, «must» donate «no less than a gold dinar.» The Gemara challenges: «But perhaps» his intention in using the word gold is not to a coin at all, but to a small «piece [naskha]» of gold. «Rabbi Elazar said:» The case of the baraita is «where he said» the word «coin.» The Gemara challenges further: «But perhaps» his intention is not to a dinar, but to smaller coins, such as «perutot. Rav Pappa said: People do not make perutot of gold.» Therefore, it is unlikely that this was his intent."
  }
 ],
